EMACS SKIA BACKEND vs CAIRO: A TECHNICAL COMPARISON
===================================================

This document compares the experimental Skia 2D graphics backend with the
traditional Cairo backend used in Emacs PGTK.


OVERVIEW
--------

Cairo (Traditional)          | Skia (New)
-----------------------------|------------------------------------
CPU-based rasterization      | GPU-accelerated via OpenGL (Ganesh)
Mature, battle-tested        | Experimental, modern
C API                        | C++ core with C wrapper API
Direct Cairo calls           | emacs_skia_* API calls


ARCHITECTURE
------------

Cairo:
  - Uses cairo_t context and cairo_surface_t
  - Font rendering via cairo-ft (FreeType integration)
  - Drawing operations are CPU-bound (see GPU INVOLVEMENT section)
  - Double-buffering managed by GTK

Skia:
  - Uses emacs_skia_canvas_t and emacs_skia_surface_t
  - Ganesh GPU backend via GrDirectContext
  - Falls back to CPU raster when GL unavailable
  - Integrates with GtkGLArea for hardware acceleration
  - Custom C API wraps Skia's C++ internals


CAIRO AND GPU INVOLVEMENT
-------------------------

A common misconception is that Cairo is purely CPU-based. The reality is
more nuanced:

Cairo's Drawing Operations (Always CPU):
  - All cairo_* drawing calls (rectangles, paths, glyphs, etc.) execute
    on the CPU using software rasterization
  - Cairo does NOT use GPU shaders for drawing primitives
  - The cairo-gl backend exists but is EXPERIMENTAL and NOT used in Emacs

Cairo's Surface Types in Emacs PGTK:
  - gdk_window_create_similar_surface() creates the drawing surface
  - On X11: CAIRO_SURFACE_TYPE_XLIB or CAIRO_SURFACE_TYPE_XCB
  - On Wayland: CAIRO_SURFACE_TYPE_IMAGE (pure software)
  - These are all CPU-rasterized surfaces

Where GPU IS Involved (Indirectly):
  1. Compositor-Level Acceleration:
     - The desktop compositor (Mutter, KWin, etc.) composites all windows
     - This compositing typically uses OpenGL/Vulkan
     - Cairo-rendered content becomes a texture that the GPU composites
     - The actual Cairo drawing was still done by CPU

  2. GTK's Display Pipeline:
     - GTK3: Widgets draw to CPU surfaces, compositor handles GPU display
     - GTK4/GSK: GskGLRenderer can GPU-composite GskCairoNode content
       (but Cairo's drawing within the node is still CPU-based)

  3. Wayland HiDPI Scaling:
     - Wayland compositors can GPU-scale Cairo-rendered content
     - This is scaling of already-rendered pixels, not GPU drawing

The Rendering Pipeline:

  Cairo (in Emacs PGTK):
    Widget draw → Cairo CPU rasterization → GDK surface → Compositor GPU → Display
                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
                  This part is always CPU

  Skia (this backend):
    Widget draw → Skia GPU rasterization → GtkGLArea FBO → Display
                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
                  This part uses GPU directly

Why This Matters:
  - Cairo: CPU does all the work; GPU only composites the final result
  - Skia: GPU does the actual drawing operations (rectangles, glyphs, etc.)
  - For text-heavy applications like Emacs, glyph rendering is the bottleneck
  - Skia can batch and GPU-accelerate glyph rendering directly

What About cairo-gl?
  - Cairo has an experimental OpenGL backend (cairo-gl)
  - It is NOT production-ready and NOT used in Emacs
  - From Cairo docs: "still experimental" since ~2010
  - Not listed in Cairo 1.18's official surface type documentation


BUILD CONFIGURATION
-------------------

Enable Skia:
  ./configure --with-pgtk --with-skia

Required defines (set automatically):
  USE_SKIA=1     - Main Skia enable flag
  SK_GL=1        - OpenGL/Ganesh backend
  SK_GANESH=1    - GPU rendering

Optional:
  SK_PDF         - PDF export support
  SK_SVG         - SVG export support


API COMPARISON
--------------

Drawing Rectangles:

  Cairo:
    cairo_t *cr = pgtk_begin_cr_clip(f);
    cairo_rectangle(cr, x, y, width, height);
    cairo_fill(cr);
    pgtk_end_cr_clip(f);

  Skia:
    emacs_skia_canvas_t *canvas = FRAME_SKIA_CANVAS(f);
    emacs_skia_paint_t *paint = FRAME_SKIA_PAINT(f);
    emacs_skia_rect_t rect = {x, y, x+width, y+height};
    emacs_skia_canvas_draw_rect(canvas, &rect, paint);

Drawing Text/Glyphs:

  Cairo:
    cairo_show_glyphs(cr, glyphs, num_glyphs);

  Skia:
    emacs_skia_canvas_draw_glyphs(canvas, count, glyphs,
                                   positions, origin, font, paint);

State Management:

  Cairo:
    cairo_save(cr);
    cairo_restore(cr);

  Skia:
    emacs_skia_canvas_save(canvas);
    emacs_skia_canvas_restore(canvas);


COLOR HANDLING
--------------

Cairo uses RGB (0xRRGGBB)
Skia uses ARGB (0xAARRGGBB)

Conversion helper:
  static inline emacs_skia_color_t
  pgtk_color_to_skia(unsigned long color)
  {
    return EMACS_SKIA_COLOR_RGB((color >> 16) & 0xff,
                                (color >> 8) & 0xff,
                                color & 0xff);
  }


FONT RENDERING
--------------

Cairo:
  - Uses cairo-ft for FreeType integration
  - cairo_scaled_font_t manages font instances
  - cairo_glyph_t for glyph representation
  - Subpixel rendering via cairo options

Skia:
  - Uses FreeType/fontconfig for font discovery
  - emacs_skia_font_t wraps SkFont
  - emacs_skia_typeface_t wraps SkTypeface
  - Dedicated font driver: skiafont.c
  - Configurable hinting: NONE, SLIGHT, NORMAL, FULL
  - Configurable antialiasing: NONE, NORMAL, SUBPIXEL
  - HarfBuzz integration via skiahbfont_driver

Font Data Structure:

  struct skia_font_info {
    struct font_info base;          // FreeType base info
    emacs_skia_typeface_t *skia_typeface;
    emacs_skia_font_t *skia_font;
  };


SURFACE TYPES
-------------

Cairo (available types):
  - cairo_image_surface_t (CPU raster)
  - cairo_xlib_surface_t (X11 via XLib)
  - cairo_xcb_surface_t (X11 via XCB)
  - cairo_recording_surface_t (vector recording)
  - cairo_pdf_surface_t, cairo_svg_surface_t
  - cairo_gl_surface_t (EXPERIMENTAL - not used in Emacs)

Cairo (actually used in Emacs PGTK):
  - Created via gdk_window_create_similar_surface()
  - X11: XLib or XCB surface (still CPU-rasterized, just uses X
    shared memory for efficient transfer)
  - Wayland: Image surface (pure CPU raster to memory buffer)
  - Export: PDF, SVG, PNG surfaces

Skia:
  - Raster surface (CPU): emacs_skia_surface_create_raster()
  - GL surface (GPU): emacs_skia_surface_create_gl()
  - Pixel-wrapped surface: emacs_skia_surface_create_from_pixels()
  - PDF document: emacs_skia_document_create_pdf()
  - SVG canvas: emacs_skia_svg_canvas_create()

Skia (actually used in Emacs):
  - GL surface via GtkGLArea (primary, GPU-accelerated)
  - Falls back to raster surface if GL unavailable


BLEND MODES
-----------

Skia supports these blend modes (similar to Cairo operators):

  EMACS_SKIA_BLEND_SRC        - Replace destination
  EMACS_SKIA_BLEND_SRC_OVER   - Normal alpha compositing
  EMACS_SKIA_BLEND_DST_OVER   - Draw behind destination
  EMACS_SKIA_BLEND_CLEAR      - Clear destination
  EMACS_SKIA_BLEND_XOR        - XOR compositing
  EMACS_SKIA_BLEND_DIFFERENCE - Color difference
  EMACS_SKIA_BLEND_EXCLUSION  - Exclusion blend


DRAWING PRIMITIVES
------------------

                      Cairo                  Skia
Rectangles            cairo_rectangle        emacs_skia_canvas_draw_rect
Lines                 cairo_line_to          emacs_skia_canvas_draw_line
Arcs                  cairo_arc              emacs_skia_canvas_draw_arc
Paths                 cairo_path_t           emacs_skia_path_t
Images                cairo_set_source_surface  emacs_skia_canvas_draw_image
Glyphs                cairo_show_glyphs      emacs_skia_canvas_draw_glyphs
Clipping              cairo_clip             emacs_skia_canvas_clip_rect


PERFORMANCE CHARACTERISTICS
---------------------------

Cairo:
  + Mature codebase, predictable performance
  + No GPU driver dependencies
  + Low memory overhead
  + Works identically everywhere (no driver variance)
  - CPU-bound for ALL drawing operations
  - Glyph rendering can bottleneck on CPU
  - Complex compositing competes with Emacs Lisp for CPU
  - X11 surfaces may have some XRender acceleration for
    simple operations (fills, blits) depending on driver

Skia:
  + GPU acceleration for most operations
  + Efficient batching of draw calls
  + Hardware-accelerated image transformations
  + Optimized glyph caching on GPU
  + Drawing doesn't compete with Emacs Lisp for CPU
  - Requires OpenGL 3.2+ support
  - Higher memory usage (GPU VRAM for textures)
  - Potential driver compatibility issues
  - GPU context switching overhead for small operations

Skia Optimizations in This Implementation:
  - FBO content preservation during resize
  - Surface snapshots for scrolling (GPU texture copy)
  - Lazy surface creation
  - Glyph metrics caching
  - Fringe bitmap caching as Skia images (uploaded once to GPU)
  - Reusable paint objects per frame
  - Direct FBO blitting via GtkGLArea

Where Skia Helps Most:
  - Scrolling large buffers (GPU texture operations)
  - Image-heavy content (transformations, scaling)
  - High-DPI displays (more pixels to rasterize)
  - Complex faces with many attributes
  - Systems where CPU is the bottleneck

Where Cairo May Be Comparable:
  - Simple text editing (glyph rendering is fast either way)
  - Small windows/low resolution
  - Systems with fast CPUs but weak GPUs
  - When compositor already provides smooth scrolling


